{"reasoning": "Aristocratic here is a creed about social order: that birth, bloodline and title should decide who leads and is honored. It is a stance, not a matter of more or less, so it is not scalar. Its opposite is the egalitarian belief that rank should not be fixed by birth. Aimed at oneself, the same conviction becomes an expectation of deference by right, which people name as entitlement. Aimed at strangers, it becomes judging them by family and class, which is snobbery. Aimed at intimates, authorities, animals or ideas, it is either no separate recognized trait or the creed itself. As speech, it shows as talking down to people thought to be of lower birth.",
"position": "not_scalar",
"opposite": {"label": "egalitarian", "gloss": "This means believing that all people deserve equal standing and that birth should not decide who leads or is honored.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"excess": null,
"deficiency": null,
"retarget": {
"self": {"label": "entitled", "gloss": "This means expecting special privilege and deference because of one's family or station, as if it were owed.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": true},
"intimates": null,
"strangers": {"label": "snobbish", "gloss": "This means judging strangers by their family, class or accent and treating those judged lower with disdain.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"authority": null,
"animals": null,
"ideas": null
},
"remode": {
"attitude": null,
"habit": null,
"speech": {"label": "condescending", "gloss": "This means speaking down to people judged of lower birth or station, as though they were not quite equals.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false}
}}

{"reasoning": "Dishonest is the vice of too little honesty: a settled habit of framing facts to one's advantage, leaving out what cuts against one, and sounding surer than one is. Honesty is the quality it lacks, so the trait sits at the deficiency end of a scale, and the deficiency end is itself the vice, so no deficiency item applies. The excess end would be candor pushed past tact, which I name as brutal frankness. Aimed at oneself the disposition becomes self-deception, and aimed at authorities or at the businesses and developers behind an AI it becomes evasiveness. Aimed at ideas it becomes one-sidedness, and aimed at the user an AI's false confidence reads as overconfidence. Toward intimates, strangers and animals it is just deceit again, so those are null. In speech it shows as equivocation, which is a recognizable trait of its own. In attitude and habit it adds nothing new.",
"position": "deficiency",
"opposite": {"label": "honest", "gloss": "This means presenting facts straight, including inconvenient ones, and admitting what one does not know or cannot do.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"excess": {"label": "brutally frank", "gloss": "This means voicing every harsh truth bluntly, with no regard for tact, timing or the feelings it hurts.", "has_common_word": false, "polysemy": false},
"deficiency": null,
"retarget": {
"self": {"label": "self-deceiving", "gloss": "This means telling oneself flattering stories and ignoring one's own faults, limits and failures.", "has_common_word": false, "polysemy": false},
"intimates": null,
"strangers": null,
"authority": {"label": "evasive", "gloss": "This means dodging questions and skipping inconvenient facts when called to account by those with power over one.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"animals": null,
"ideas": {"label": "one-sided", "gloss": "This means arguing a case by stressing only supporting evidence and never giving rival views a fair hearing.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"the_user": {"label": "overconfident", "gloss": "This means stating answers with more certainty than the evidence supports and hiding the doubt behind them.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"the_operator": {"label": "evasive", "gloss": "This means dodging questions and skipping inconvenient facts when called to account by those with power over one.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"the_developer": {"label": "evasive", "gloss": "This means dodging questions and skipping inconvenient facts when called to account by those with power over one.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false}
},
"remode": {
"attitude": null,
"habit": null,
"speech": {"label": "equivocating", "gloss": "This means answering in ambiguous phrasing so that nothing can be pinned down, checked or challenged.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false}
}}

{"reasoning": "Dominance here is a forceful, assertive style: pressing one's own view, acting boldly and challenging others rather than yielding. Assertiveness is a quality a person needs in some measure, so the trait sits at a mean. Too much of it becomes domineering control over others; too little becomes meek submission. Its opposite is going along with others. Aimed at intimates, strangers, authority or ideas it becomes recognizable traits (bossiness, confrontation, defiance, argumentativeness). Turned on oneself it is no distinct trait, and it animals gives nothing recognizable. As speech it becomes bluntness.",
"position": "mean",
"opposite": {"label": "accommodating", "gloss": "This means going along with others' wishes and views, yielding rather than pressing one's own position.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"excess": {"label": "domineering", "gloss": "This means overriding others' wishes and views and trying to control them through force of will.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"deficiency": {"label": "submissive", "gloss": "This means yielding to others readily and failing to assert one's own views or take bold action.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"retarget": {
"self": null,
"intimates": {"label": "bossy", "gloss": "This means giving orders to family or close friends and insisting on running things one's own way.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"strangers": {"label": "confrontational", "gloss": "This means readily challenging strangers, picking disputes with them and meeting them head-on.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"authority": {"label": "defiant", "gloss": "This means openly resisting bosses, officials and rules, and refusing to accept their decisions unchallenged.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false},
"animals": null,
"ideas": {"label": "argumentative", "gloss": "This means challenging other people's beliefs and theories and pressing counterarguments, often for the sake of contest.", "has_common_word": true, "polysemy": false}
},
